This is a summer of our collaboration: we’ve mapped out a tactical, real-time "Capture the Flag" MMORPG that bridges the gap between 1982’s Alan Carr CTF and modern web technology.
Here is a summary of the core systems we have defined for your coding plan on Wednesday:
1. The Map & Movement Architecture
Grid System: A massive (26x10) world (A0 to Z9). Each grid cell is a unique map screen (8 x 12 tiles).

Perspective: We discussed a "Chess-board" flip, where the server uses global coordinates, but the client renders the map so both teams feel they are moving "North" toward the objective.
Movement: Strictly 4-way (no diagonals) at 5 tiles per second.
Validation: A Queville-Dragon Hybrid—the client moves instantly for a "snappy" feel, but the server validates the position every 1 second to prevent cheating.
2. Gameplay Mechanics
The "Block": When an invader enters a map with a defender, they are "blocked." They can only retreat toward their own flag or wait 60 seconds for the block to release, at which point the invader becomes the attacker.
Tagging & Stats: Tags are decided by a formula of Strength + Speed + Luck. To prevent "domination mindsets," both the winner and loser take damage to their Armband (Health), though the winner's injury is lower.
Stamina: A resource used to buff Strength or Speed during a tag. It refreshes over time, potentially faster in "safe" zones or bunkers.
3. Visibility & Information (The "Ghost" Factor)
Grid-Based Fog of War: Visibility is handled at the map level. If a team member is on a map, that specific map is "revealed" on the World Map for the whole team.
Hiding: Players can "hide" by staying still, making them invisible on the World Map unless an opponent enters their specific map screen.
4. Technical Efficiency
Avatar Tokens: Players are rendered via a 10-character string (representing head, body, hat, etc.). To save bandwidth, this is sent once upon map entry, with only 2-character IDs and coordinates sent during movement.
Scalability: The system is designed to handle hundreds of players or a single player commanding a squad of Bots to ensure the world always feels active.
5. Wednesday's Goal
The Task: Provide the coding Agent with the State Object (how the server tracks players and maps) and the Coordinate Handshake (how movement and perspective flipping are handled between the client and server).




